": [{"name": "Transylvania, Romania", "role": "Setting of Dracula's Castle"}, {"name": "London, United Kingdom", "role": "Major Setting"}, {"name": "Whitby, United Kingdom", "role": "Key Location in the Novel"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Dracula", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "dracula"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q41542", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dracula"], "tags": ["literary work", "Novels & Prose Fiction", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Dracula left a notable mark on Modern history, documented across 75 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Dracula reshaped the horror genre and influenced cultural representations of vampires, leading to a proliferation of adaptations that continue to resonate in modern media.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-08T14:00:23Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27142557986", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "1897 novel by bram stoker Created by Bram Stoker. Created c. 1890.", "newValue": "Dracula, a fictional character created by Irish author Bram Stoker in his 1897 novel, has become an enduring symbol of vampiric lore and Gothic horror. The novel, set in Transylvania and England, introduces Count Dracula as an aristocratic vampire who seeks to move from his castle in the Carpathian Mountains to England to spread the undead curse. Stoker's work not only popularized the vampire genre but also reflected societal fears of the unknown, sexuality, and immigration during the Victorian era, making the character a crucial figure in the landscape of modern literature and culture.

The narrative of 'Dracula' unfolds through a series of journal entries, letters, and newspaper clippings, providing a multi-faceted perspective on the events. The story follows Jonathan Harker, Mina Murray, and Professor Abraham Van Helsing as they confront Dracula's malignant influence. Harker's initial visit to Dracula's castle in 1890 marks the beginning of a series of tragic events leading to the vampire's influence over Mina and others. Stoker's portrayal of Dracula as both a monstrous and charismatic figure challenges Victorian norms, encapsulating the era's anxieties about modernity and the threats it posed to traditional values.

